1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,470
Can you show me Services, service discovery and DNS?

2
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:14,562
A Service gives a group of Pods one stable address and name,

3
00:00:14,562 --> 00:00:18,487
and spreads the traffic sent to it over all of them.

4
00:00:18,487 --> 00:00:23,318
The Pods behind it are chosen with a label selector (lesson 07).

5
00:00:23,318 --> 00:00:29,130
Kubernetes' DNS turns the Service's name (backend-service) into that address,

6
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:32,149
so applications call each other by name.

7
00:00:32,149 --> 00:00:32,753
Analogy:

8
00:00:32,753 --> 00:00:35,093
a company's switchboard number.

9
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:36,891
Pod IPs change.

10
00:00:36,891 --> 00:00:46,116
Every Pod gets a new IP when it is recreated (a crash, a rolling update, scaling), and there are several Pods.

11
00:00:46,116 --> 00:00:51,148
A frontend cannot keep a list of backend Pod IPs up to date.

12
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:55,319
And how does it actually work?

13
00:00:55,867 --> 00:01:04,187
The Service's selector finds the Pods; only ready Pods (readiness probe, lesson 14) receive traffic.

14
00:01:04,187 --> 00:01:11,177
The list of current Pod addresses is stored in EndpointSlices, updated continuously.

15
00:01:11,177 --> 00:01:11,676
Types:

16
00:01:11,676 --> 00:01:22,992
| Type | Reachable from | Use | |---|---|---| | ClusterIP (default) | inside the cluster only | service-to-service traffic (the backend,

17
00:01:22,992 --> 00:01:26,736
the database) | | NodePort | every node's IP,

18
00:01:26,736 --> 00:01:30,647
on a port 30000–32767 | simple external access,

19
00:01:30,647 --> 00:01:44,210
tests, labs | | LoadBalancer | an external load balancer the cloud creates | public services in the cloud (Minikube simulates it with minikube tunnel) | DNS names:

20
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:51,532
backend-service (same namespace), backend-service.services-lab (from another namespace),

21
00:01:51,532 --> 00:01:56,441
backend-service.services-lab.svc.cluster.local (full name).

